Howdy, y'all.

This is from p146 of the 2007 Michael Joseph hardback edition of 

_Soon I Will Be Invincible_ by Austin Grossman (which is 

a jolly fun superhero romp, full of hidden references to 

all things comixy - this bit is from the point of view 

of Doctor Impossible, the World's 4th Smartest Man and 

mondo supervillain, as he reminisces about his life 

before he got his powers):

"I wrote code for computer games I ran on the primitive 

mainframe the school had, partners at chess, and even a 

dungeon game, where I steered a tiny swordsman or wizard 

through endlessly layer-caked levels that spiralled into 

the earth, sunken ballrooms and throne rooms and 

treasure houses giving way to caverns, grottoes, and 

lightless oceans, and still deeper caverns below those.

I extended them as I played, everything getting stranger 

the father down I went, from goblins and wolves to giant 

ants, dragons, and demons, and castles underground. I 

still play it occasionally, in the off-hours. There was 

never any sense who had dug that deep, or why, or when I 

was going to find the real bottom, but I never wanted to 

stop, knowing that a real prize rested there, a 

centuries-old glittering treasure or hidden revelation, 

buried fathoms-deep under stone and earth; a relic from 

the deepest past, precious as life and ancient as 

childhood memory."

...I know it could be most any roguelike, but this does 

look like a tip o'the hat to Nethack to me!

Anyway, it amused me, so I thought I'd share.

